cal development, but doesn’t block the diagonal.\n- J10 is free.\n\nSo extending the diagonal from H8–I9 to **J10** would make three in a row: H8–I9–J10.\n\nBut wait — is that correct?\n- H8 → col H=8, row 8\n- I9 → col I=9, row 9\n- J10 → col J=10, row 10 → yes, consistent\n\nSo playing **J10** would form a 3-stone diagonal, opening up a strong threat.\n\nIs there a better move?\n\nAlternative directions from I9:\n- Horizontal: I-file → H9, J9 → H9 is empty, J9 is empty\n- Vertical: I9 → I8, I10 → I8 is empty, I10 is blocked by White\n- Other diagonal (/): H10, G11, etc. → no support\n\nSo the strongest potential is the \\ diagonal: H8–I9–[J10]–K11–L12\n\nPlaying **J10** builds a 3-in-a-row with potential for open-ended growth.\n\nBut is there a **winning move**? That is, can Black place a stone to complete **five in a row** immediately? Clearly not — only two stones exist.\n\nCan Black create a **lethal threat**, like an *open four* or *double three*?\n\nNot yet — but **J10** is the natural move to build momentum.\n\nAre there any threats from White?\n- White at I10 could try to build downward: I11, I12, etc., but only one stone\n- E14 is isolated\n\nNo immediate danger.\n\nThus, best move is to extend the existing diagonal.\n\nCould we play **G7**? That’s behind H8 — G7 would be col 7, row 7 → also on the same diagonal (G7-H8-I9), so that’s another way to extend backward.\n\nSo options:\n- **J10**: forward extension of diagonal → creates 3-in-a-row: G7-H8-I9-J10 would be 4 if G7 is filled later\n- **G7**: backward extension → also forms 3-in-a-row: G7-H8-I9\n\nWhich is better?\n\nBoth create 3-in-a-row. But **J10** advances toward the center and opens more space ahead (J10, K11, L12, M13, N14, O15), while G7 only adds backward.\n\nAlso, **J10** may be more threatening because it pushes forward, potentially forcing White to respond.\n\nMoreover, **J10 is not occupied** — confirmed: Occupied are H8, I9, I10, E14 → J10 is free.\n\nSimilarly, G7 is free.\n\nBut strategically, advancing is better than retreating when equal.\n\nAnother idea: **H9**? That would form a horizontal or vertical link?\n- H9: same row as H8? No — H8 is row 8, H9 is row 9 → same column? Yes, vertical on H-column\n- But H8 is at row 8, H9 would be row 9 → but Black has no stone at H9\n- But I9 is at row 9 — so H9 would connect horizontally with I9\n- So H9 would create a horizontal pair: H9–I9\n- Also vertical: H8–H9\n\nSo H9 would form a **knight-like shape**, connecting both H8 and I9.\n\nThat is: placing at H9 would result in:\n- Vertical: H8–H9 (2)\n- Horizontal: H9–I9 (2)\n- And possibly diagonal later\n\nBut more importantly, it would form a **cluster** around H8-I9, creating multiple threats.\n\nIn fact, **H9** connects both existing Black stones into a triangle — very strong.\n\nLet’s visualize:\n- After H9:\n  - H8 (X), H9 (X), I9 (X)\n  - So vertical H8–H9, horizontal H9–I9, and diagonal H8–I9\n\nThis creates a **fork** of threats: vertical, horizontal, and diagonal all developing from one point.\n\nCompared to J10:\n- J10 gives a straight diagonal of 3: H8–I9–J10\n- But H9 gives a central hub that links and strengthens both stones, enabling multiple directions\n\nIn professional Gomoku, such **central joining moves** are often superior because they increase connectivity and create dual-direction threats.\n\nMoreover, leaving H9 open risks White playing there and splitting our stones.\n\nTherefore, **H9 is a better move than J10 or G7**.\n\nNow check if H9 is occupied:  \nOccupied: H8, I9, I10, E14 → H9 is **free**\n\nSo H9 is available.\n\nIs there any other candidate?\n\nWhat about **G8**? That’s adjacent to H8 — would form horizontal G8–H8 or diagonal G8–I9? G8 to I9 is two steps right and one up — not aligned.\n\nG8 would only connect leftward from H8 — less valuable.\n\nSimilarly, J9: adjacent to I9 — horizontal extension, but isolated from H8 unless H9 is played.\n\nSo again, **H9** is the optimal junction.\n\nConclusion: Play **H9** to connect H8 and I9 into a flexible, multi-directional formation.\n\nReason: Connecting the two existing black stones at H8 and I9 via H9 creates a strong central position with simultaneous threats in vertical (H8-H9), horizontal (H9-I9), and diagonal (H8-I9) directions. This maximizes future attacking potential and prevents White from exploiting the gap. It is more strategic than extending only one direction.\n\nAnswer: {"col": "H", "row": 9}'}
